## Better, But Still Broken: While, Not If

Fortunately, this fix is easy (Figure 30.10): change the if to a while. Think about why this works; now consumer T<sub>c</sub>1 wakes up and (with the lock held) immediately re-checks the state of the shared variable (c2). If the buffer is empty at that point, the consumer simply goes back to sleep (c3). The corollary if is also changed to a while in the producer (p2).

Thanks to Mesa semantics, a simple rule to remember with condition variables is to always use while loops. Sometimes you don’t have to recheck the condition, but it is always safe to do so; just do it and be happy.

